==Background==
{{See also|Allen v. Milligan|2022 United States House of Representatives elections in Alabama#Background}}

===''Allen v. Milligan''===
During the [[2020 United States redistricting cycle|2020 redistricting cycle]], Alabama's congressional map faced legal challenges for alleged violations of the [[Voting Rights Act of 1965]]. 27% of Alabama's population is African American, but only one of Alabama's seven districts was drawn with a Black majority. A federal panel initially blocked the 2022 map, finding that the state illegally discriminated against Black voters by not drawing a second majority-Black district.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Chandler |first1=Kim |title=Alabama's new congressional districts map blocked by judges |url=https://apnews.com/article/voting-rights-alabama-race-and-ethnicity-legislature-redistricting-911cfdda84f55956e15a189782d319bb |website=Associated Press |date=January 25, 2022 |access-date=January 25, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Alabama's new congressional map blocked by judges |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2022/01/24/alabama-redistricting-map-rejected-00001687 |website=Politico |date=24 January 2022 |access-date=January 25, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220126203016/https://www.politico.com/news/2022/01/24/alabama-redistricting-map-rejected-00001687 |archive-date=January 26, 2022 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="NYT">{{Cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/01/24/us/politics/alabama-congressional-map-redistricting.html|title=Court Throws Out Alabama's New Congressional Map|last=Epstein|first=Reid J.|work=The New York Times|date=January 24, 2022|accessdate=January 26, 2022}}</ref> However, on February 7, 2022, the [[Supreme Court of the United States]] granted a stay on the case that had been requested by the state of Alabama, allowing the map to remain in place for the [[2022 United States House of Representatives elections in Alabama|2022 elections]], but signaled it would fully review the case at a later date.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Liptak |first1=Adam |title=Supreme Court, in 5-4 Vote, Restores Alabama's Congressional Voting Map|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/02/07/us/politics/supreme-court-alabama-redistricting-congressional-map.html|website=The New York Times|date=February 7, 2022 |access-date=February 7, 2022}}</ref><ref name="msnbc scotus stay">{{cite web | url = https://www.msnbc.com/rachel-maddow-show/maddowblog/conservative-justices-rescue-alabamas-racially-discriminatory-map-rcna15316 | title = Conservative justices rescue Alabama's racially discriminatory map | first = Steve | last= Benen |author-link=Steve Benen | date = February 8, 2022 | accessdate = February 8, 2022 | work = [[MSNBC]] }}</ref>

On June 8, 2023, the Supreme Court affirmed the previous finding that the map discriminated against Black Alabamians. In a 5–4 decision in ''[[Allen v. Milligan]]'', Justice [[Brett Kavanaugh]] and Chief Justice [[John Roberts]] joined the Supreme Court's liberal wing (Justices [[Ketanji Brown Jackson]], [[Elena Kagan]], and [[Sonia Sotomayor]]) and ruled in favor of Milligan. The decision, considered an unexpected victory for voting rights activists in Alabama, upheld the federal panel's ruling that Alabama had illegally diluted the power of Black voters.<ref>{{cite news|last=Liptak|first=Adam|title= Supreme Court Rejects Voting Map That Diluted Black Voters' Power|date=Jun 8, 2023|newspaper=The New York Times|url= https://www.nytimes.com/2023/06/08/us/supreme-court-voting-rights-act-alabama.html}}</ref><ref name="NBC">{{Cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/supreme-court/supreme-court-strikes-alabama-congressional-map-voting-rights-dispute-rcna64476|title=Supreme Court backs landmark voting rights law, strikes down Alabama congressional map|last=Hurley|first=Lawrence|work=NBC News|date=June 8, 2023|access-date=June 8, 2023}}</ref> The decision forced Alabama to reconfigure its congressional districts in advance of the 2024 elections, including drawing a second predominantly Black district.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Gerstein |first1=Josh |last2=Montellaro |first2=Zach |date=June 8, 2023 |title=Voting Rights Act dodges bullet at Supreme Court |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2023/06/08/voting-rights-act-dodges-bullet-at-supreme-court-00101004 |website=Politico}}</ref> The [[Alabama Legislature]] was required to convene a special legislative session in order to draw a new map.<ref name="1819a">{{Cite web|url=https://1819news.com/news/item/algop-disappointed-in-scotus-redistricting-ruling-wahl-vows-to-work-hard-to-win-all-seven-congressional-seats|title=ALGOP 'disappointed' in SCOTUS redistricting ruling; Wahl vows to 'work hard to win all seven congressional seats'|last=Taylor|first=Caleb|work=[[1819 News]]|date=June 8, 2023|access-date=June 8, 2023}}</ref> [[John Wahl]], chair of the [[Alabama Republican Party]], published a statement in reaction to the ruling, stating that the party would "work hard to win all seven congressional seats".<ref name="1819a" />

===Redistricting process===
[[File:Alabama Congressional Districts, 119th Congress.svg|thumb|right|The new congressional districts map chosen by the trial court<br>{{maplink|from=Alabama's 1st congressional district (2025–).map|from2=Alabama's 2nd congressional district (2025–).map|from3=Alabama's 3rd congressional district (2025–).map|from4=Alabama's 4th congressional district (2025–).map|from5=Alabama's 5th congressional district (2025–).map|from6=Alabama's 6th congressional district (2025–).map|from7=Alabama's 7th congressional district (2025–).map|text=Interactive map version}}]]
A special session of the [[Alabama Legislature]] was called to approve a new congressional map in July 2023, with a deadline of July 21. The [[Alabama House of Representatives]] and [[Alabama Senate]] passed two different congressional maps separately, neither of which included a second majority-minority district. The Alabama House of Representatives passed a map proposed by Republican Representative [[Chris Pringle (politician)|Chris Pringle]] that increased the Black voting age population in [[Alabama's 2nd congressional district]] to 42%, but still below the majority-minority threshold. The Alabama Senate passed a map proposed by Republican Senator [[Steve Livingston]] that increased it to only 38%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.alreporter.com/2023/07/20/house-senate-pass-separate-congressional-maps-but-anticipate-a-compromise/|title=House, Senate pass separate congressional maps but anticipate a compromise|last=Holmes|first=Jacob|work=Alabama Political Reporter|date=July 20, 2023|access-date=July 20, 2023}}</ref> A special conference committee consisting of six members from both wings of the Alabama Legislature passed a new map with additional changes on July 21.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://1819news.com/news/item/conference-committee-passes-new-redistricting-map|title=Conference committee passes new redistricting map|last=Taylor|first=Caleb|work=1819 News|date=July 21, 2023|access-date=July 22, 2023}}</ref> The new congressional map was signed into law by Governor [[Kay Ivey]] the same day.<ref name="1819b">{{Cite web|url=https://1819news.com/news/item/new-congressional-redistricting-map-for-alabama-signed-into-law|title=New congressional redistricting map for Alabama signed into law|last=Taylor|first=Caleb|work=1819 News|date=July 21, 2023|access-date=July 22, 2023}}</ref> In the legislature's map, the Black voting age population in [[Alabama's 7th congressional district]] was reduced from 55.6% to 50.6%, while [[Alabama's 2nd congressional district]]'s Black voting age population was increased to 39.9%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/07/21/politics/alabama-congressional-map/index.html|title=Alabama GOP governor approves congressional map with just one majority-Black district despite court order|last=Gallagher|first=Diane|work=CNN|date=July 21, 2023|access-date=July 22, 2023}}</ref>

The map enacted by the state was struck down on September 5, confirming speculation by Black lawmakers in the state that it failed to comply with the requirement for a second majority Black district.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/alabama-voting-rights-redistricting-black-lawmakers-31a8b5dc4972503026e985970f5d4045|title=Black lawmakers say Alabama GOP's proposed new congressional map insults the Supreme Court|last=Chandler|first=Kim|work=Associated Press|date=July 19, 2023|access-date=July 20, 2023}}</ref> The panel of judges ruling on the maps wrote that they were "deeply troubled that the State enacted a map that the State readily admits does not provide the remedy we said federal law requires". The judges appointed a [[special master]], Richard Allen (no relation to [[Wes Allen (politician)|Wes Allen]] or ''Allen v. Milligan''),<ref name="ARSept25">{{Cite web|url=https://alabamareflector.com/2023/09/25/alabama-redistricting-special-master-submits-three-proposed-congressional-maps/|title=Alabama redistricting: Special master submits three proposed congressional maps|last1=Lyman|first1=Brian|last2=Stephenson|first2=Jemma|work=Alabama Reflector|date=September 25, 2023|access-date=September 25, 2023}}</ref> to take control of the mapmaking process following Alabama lawmakers' defiance.<ref>{{cite web|last=Lo Wang|first=Hansi|title=Alabama's congressional map is struck down again for diluting Black voters' power|date=Sep 5, 2023|website=NPR|url= https://www.npr.org/2023/09/05/1193749552/alabama-congressional-map}}</ref> Three proposed maps from the special master were released on September 25. In all of the maps, [[Coffee County, Alabama|Coffee County]], which was represented by and home to [[Barry Moore (American politician)|Barry Moore]], was moved to [[Alabama's 1st congressional district]], which set up a primary in which [[Jerry Carl]] and Moore were both incumbents pitted against each other.<ref name="ARSept25" /><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://1819news.com/news/item/special-master-releases-proposed-redistricting-maps-placing-moore-carl-in-same-district|title=Special master releases proposed redistricting maps placing Moore, Carl in same district|last=Taylor|first=Daniel|work=1819 News|date=September 25, 2023|access-date=September 25, 2023}}</ref>

===Finalization of special master map===
The state of Alabama, under [[Attorney General of Alabama]] [[Steve Marshall (politician)|Steve Marshall]], requested a stay on the federal panel's decision to not allow the legislature's new map. On September 26, 2023, the Supreme Court again denied Alabama's request, meaning that the special master's map was to be used in the 2024 elections. All three of the proposed remedial maps raised the Black voting-age population in the second congressional district in ranges from 48.5% to 50.1%.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://1819news.com/news/item/scotus-denies-alabamas-request-for-stay-on-federal-courts-redistricting-ruling|title=SCOTUS denies Alabama's request for stay on federal court's redistricting ruling|last=Taylor|first=Caleb|work=1819 News|date=September 26, 2023|access-date=September 26, 2023}}</ref> The Alabama Democratic Conference, the Black caucus of the [[Alabama Democratic Party]], also announced the same day that it planned to file an objection to the special master's maps, saying that they did not go far enough to create a majority-minority district. The conference offered its own redistricting map in the objection,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.al.com/politics/2023/09/alabama-democratic-conference-to-file-objection-to-special-master-offer-own-redistricting-map.html|title=Alabama Democratic Conference to file objection to special master, offer own redistricting map|last=Koplowitz|first=Howard|work=AL.com|date=September 26, 2023|access-date=September 26, 2023}}</ref> but it was denied by the federal panel.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://alabamareflector.com/briefs/federal-court-denies-intervention-by-joe-reed-led-group-in-alabama-redistricting-case/|title=Federal court denies intervention by Joe Reed-led group in Alabama redistricting case|last=Stephenson|first=Jemma|work=Alabama Reflector|date=July 26, 2023|access-date=March 5, 2024}}</ref>

Following another hearing on the case, the federal panel issued an opinion on October 5, 2023, ordering Alabama to implement the congressional map known as Remedial Plan 3, as drawn by the special master. [[Secretary of State of Alabama]] [[Wes Allen (politician)|Wes Allen]] said his office would implement the new map for the 2024 elections. In Remedial Plan 3, Alabama's 2nd congressional district has a Black voting-age population of 48.7%, and a Black-preferred candidate was found to have won in 16 of 17 simulated elections.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://alabamareflector.com/2023/10/05/federal-court-selects-new-alabama-congressional-map/|title=Federal court selects new Alabama congressional map|last1=Lyman|first1=Brian|last2=Stephenson|first2=Jemma|work=Alabama Reflector|date=October 5, 2023|access-date=October 5, 2023}}</ref>

==Overview==
[[File:Alabama-house-119th-congress-demographics.svg|thumb|right|250px|Demographic map of the districts]]
Republicans won five<ref name="politico_alabama">{{cite web |title=Alabama House election results |url=https://www.politico.com/2024-election/results/alabama/house/ |website=Politico |access-date=22 November 2024}}</ref> districts, while Democrats won two: the newly created<ref name="al_02_guardian">{{cite news |last1=Walker |first1=Adria |title='Excitement in the air': newly created Alabama district votes for first time |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/05/new-alabama-congressional-district-two |access-date=22 November 2024 |agency=The Guardian |date=5 November 2024}}</ref> second one and the existing seventh one. Both those districts are plurality Black, whereas the ones by Republicans are predominantly white.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Walker |first1=Chris |title=Shomari Figures Wins in District Where Alabama GOP Tried to Dilute Black Vote |url=https://truthout.org/articles/shomari-figures-wins-in-district-where-alabama-gop-tried-to-dilute-black-vote/ |access-date=22 November 2024 |agency=truthout |date=7 November 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Who Will Represent Alabama's New Democratic District? |url=https://prospect.org/politics/2024-01-29-alabamas-new-democratic-district/ |access-date=22 November 2024 |agency=The American Prospect |date=29 January 2024}}</ref> Such a correlation is consistent<ref>{{cite news |title=Surprise Supreme Court win for Black voters could give Democrats an electoral boost |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-election/surprise-supreme-court-win-black-voters-give-democrats-electoral-boost-rcna88358 |access-date=22 November 2024 |agency=NBC News |date=9 June 2023}}</ref> with a preference towards Democrats among Alabama's Black voters.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Willis |first1=Alexander |title=Alabama GOP makes 'historic' gains with minority voters in 2024 election |url=https://aldailynews.com/alabama-gop-makes-historic-gains-with-minority-voters-in-2024-election/ |access-date=22 November 2024 |agency=Alabama Daily News |date=22 November 2024}}</ref> Democrats fielded their candidates in four districts out of seven, with Republicans securing almost unchallenged victories in the third, fourth and fifth districts. Outside of those, Republicans earned their best result in the first district, with 78.4% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web |title=Alabama House Results 2024 |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2024-elections/alabama-house-results |website=NBC News |access-date=22 November 2024}}</ref>

Republicans improved on their 2022 result, earning 3.5% of the vote more.<ref>{{cite web |title=Alabama U.S. House Election Results 2022 |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2022-elections/alabama-house-results |website=NBC News |access-date=24 November 2024}}</ref> However, this election became the first since [[2008 United States House of Representatives elections in Alabama|2008]] when Democrats held more than a single seat in Alabama.<ref>{{cite web |title=Alabama Presidential Election Voting History |url=https://www.270towin.com/states/Alabama |website=270 to Win |access-date=24 November 2024}}</ref> Furthermore, this marked the first time when two Black Representatives were simultaneously elected from the state.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Stephenson |first1=Jemma |title=For 1st time in history, Alabama will have two Black U.S. House members serving together |url=https://alabamareflector.com/2024/11/11/alabama-will-have-two-black-u-s-house-members-serving-together-for-1st-time-in-history/ |access-date=24 November 2024 |agency=Alabama Reflector |date=11 November 2024}}</ref>
