{{see also|Alabama's 4th congressional district}}
The 4th district is located in rural north-central Alabama, including [[Colbert County, Alabama|Colbert]], [[Cullman County, Alabama|Cullman]], [[Fayette County, Alabama|Fayette]], and [[Marion County, Alabama|Marion]] counties, as well as half of [[Lauderdale County, Alabama|Lauderdale]] and [[Tuscaloosa County, Alabama|Tuscaloosa]] counties. [[Blount County, Alabama|Blount County]] was also re-added to the district with the new map.<ref name="Aderholt1">{{Cite web|url=https://1819news.com/news/item/u-s-rep-aderholt-on-redistricting-the-court-was-speaking-out-of-both-sides-of-its-mouth|title=Aderholt on redistricting: 'The Court was speaking out of both sides of its mouth'|last=Taylor|first=Caleb|work=1819 News|date=September 27, 2023|access-date=September 27, 2023}}</ref> It includes the cities of [[Cullman, Alabama|Cullman]], [[Haleyville, Alabama|Haleyville]], [[Jasper, Alabama|Jasper]], and [[Muscle Shoals, Alabama|Muscle Shoals]]. In 2022, according to the [[Cook Partisan Voting Index]], it was the most Republican district in the country, with an index rating of R+33.<ref name="2022 Report">{{cite web |last1=Wasserman |first1=David |title=Introducing the 2022 Cook Partisan Voting Index (Cook PVI) |url=https://www.cookpolitical.com/cook-pvi/2022-partisan-voting-index/introducing-2022-cook-partisan-voting-index |website=The Cook Political Report with Amy Walter |date=July 13, 2022 |access-date=July 13, 2022}}</ref> The incumbent is Republican [[Robert Aderholt]], who was re-elected with 84.2% of the vote in 2022.<ref name="HPVT"/>

No Democratic candidates qualified to run in this district, though Aderholt faced one unsuccessful primary challenger, Justin Holcomb.<ref name="WhoALNov" />

