{{see also|Alabama's 5th congressional district}}
The 5th district is based in northern Alabama, including the city of [[Huntsville, Alabama|Huntsville]], as well as [[Athens, Alabama|Athens]], [[Decatur, Alabama|Decatur]], [[Madison, Alabama|Madison]], and [[Scottsboro, Alabama|Scottsboro]], as well as half of [[Lauderdale County, Alabama|Lauderdale County]].<ref name="Aderholt1" /> The incumbent is first-term Republican [[Dale Strong]], who was elected with 67.2% of the vote in 2022.<ref name="HPVT"/>

No Democratic candidates qualified to run in this district. Strong initially faced one primary challenger, former [[Alabama House of Representatives|state representative]] [[Daniel Boman]], who was a member of the Democratic Party during most of his tenure, but switched back to the Republican Party after leaving office.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://1819news.com/news/item/21-candidates-qualify-for-congressional-district-2-including-former-alabama-and-nfl-defensive-end-wallace-gilberry|title=21 candidates qualify for congressional District 2 including former Alabama and NFL defensive end Wallace Gilberry|last=Taylor|first=Caleb|work=1819 News|date=November 11, 2023|access-date=November 12, 2023}}</ref> Boman faced a challenge to his candidacy from within the [[Alabama Republican Party]]; he was officially removed from the ballot in December 2023. This effectively left Strong unopposed in 2024.<ref name="ALBoman">{{Cite web|url=https://www.al.com/news/2023/12/alabama-gop-removes-candidate-daniel-boman-from-ballot-in-5th-district.html|title=Alabama GOP removes candidate Daniel Boman from ballot in 5th District|last=Cason|first=Mike|work=AL.com|date=December 11, 2023|access-date=December 12, 2023}}</ref>

