The 6th district encompasses the central part of the state near [[Birmingham metropolitan area, Alabama|Greater Birmingham]], taking in the northeastern parts of the city of [[Birmingham, Alabama|Birmingham]] and [[Jefferson County, Alabama|Jefferson County]], as well as the surrounding suburbs, including [[Bibb County, Alabama|Bibb]], [[Chilton County, Alabama|Chilton]], [[Coosa County, Alabama|Coosa]], and [[Shelby County, Alabama|Shelby]] counties. Other cities include [[Alabaster, Alabama|Alabaster]], [[Hoover, Alabama|Hoover]] and [[Montevallo, Alabama|Montevallo]]. The incumbent is Republican [[Gary Palmer (politician)|Gary Palmer]], who was re-elected with 84.7% of the vote in 2022 against a [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]] candidate.<ref name="HPVT"/>

Palmer's re-election campaign gained attention due to his 2014 signing of the [[U.S. Term Limits]] Pledge and campaign promise to not run for more than five terms to Congress. Palmer's previous statements meant that he would have retired in 2024, however, Palmer chose to seek a sixth term in this election.<ref name="ALTPalmer">{{Cite web|url=https://altoday.com/archives/50389-gary-palmer-breaking-key-campaign-promise-after-praying-to-god-for-clarity|title=Gary Palmer breaking key campaign promise after praying to God for 'clarity'|last=Moseley|first=Brandon|work=Alabama Today|date=March 11, 2023|access-date=March 11, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240515143253/https://altoday.com/archives/50389-gary-palmer-breaking-key-campaign-promise-after-praying-to-god-for-clarity|archive-date=May 15, 2024|url-status=dead}}</ref> Palmer cited his reasons for seeking re-election, including recent high turnover in [[Alabama's congressional delegations|Alabama's congressional delegation]], his rise to Republican leadership within the House of Representatives,<ref name="WilkinsCohen">{{Cite web|url=https://about.bgov.com/news/gop-maps-out-next-agenda-with-aid-of-quiet-lawmaker-from-alabama/|title=GOP Maps Out Next Agenda With Aid of Quiet Lawmaker From Alabama|last1=Wilkins|first1=Emily|last2=Cohen|first2=Zach|work=Bloomberg Government|date=March 4, 2022|access-date=December 12, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240717102820/https://about.bgov.com/news/gop-maps-out-next-agenda-with-aid-of-quiet-lawmaker-from-alabama/|archive-date=July 17, 2024|url-status=dead}}</ref> and personal prayer, saying that he had "prayed for God to give me clarity on it".<ref name="ALTPalmer" /> He disputed media reports (including an article by ''AL.com'') that characterized his five-term limit as being part of the U.S. Term Limits pledge, when in fact, the pledge only applied to sponsoring legislation. However, Palmer acknowledged that he did claim during his 2014 campaign that he would serve no more than five terms, and said he would "own that", regarding breaking that campaign promise.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://1819news.com/news/item/u-s-rep-palmer-running-again-in-2024-claims-pledge-was-to-support-term-limit-legislation-but-acknowledges-saying-would-only-serve-five-terms|title=U.S. Rep. Palmer running again in 2024; Claims pledge was to support term-limit legislation, but acknowledges saying would only serve five terms|last=Blakely|first=Will|work=1819 News|date=March 14, 2023|access-date=March 14, 2023}}</ref>

