{{see also|Alabama's 7th congressional district}}
The 7th district encompasses the west-central part of the state in the [[Black Belt (region of Alabama)|Black Belt]], including the cities of [[Demopolis, Alabama|Demopolis]], [[Greensboro, Alabama|Greensboro]], and [[Selma, Alabama|Selma]], as well as taking in majority-black areas of [[Birmingham, Alabama|Birmingham]] and [[Tuscaloosa, Alabama|Tuscaloosa]]. The incumbent is Democrat [[Terri Sewell]], who was re-elected with 63.6% of the vote in 2022.<ref name="HPVT"/>

Sewell is running for reelection to an eighth term.<ref name="Sewell">{{Cite web|url=https://docquery.fec.gov/cgi-bin/forms/H0AL07086/1675615/|title=Terri Sewell FEC Statement of Candidacy|access-date=March 7, 2023}}</ref><ref name="Sewell2024">{{cite news |last1=Lesniewski |first1=Niels |last2=McIntire |first2=Mary Ellen |title=Alabama ruling could help Democrats in multiple states |url=https://rollcall.com/2023/06/08/alabama-ruling-could-help-democrats-in-multiple-states/ |work=Roll Call |date=June 8, 2023 |quote=Sewell, who lives in Birmingham, said she expects to run again in the 7th District, but acknowledged that her district could change under a new map.}}</ref> [[Alabama Senate|State senator]] and Senate Minority Leader [[Bobby Singleton (politician)|Bobby Singleton]] formed an exploratory committee to run against Sewell,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://aldailynews.com/singleton-considering-congressional-run-against-sewell/|title=Singleton considering congressional run against Sewell|work=Alabama Daily News|date=September 26, 2023|access-date=November 12, 2023}}</ref> but ultimately did not enter the race. However, Sewell did face one unsuccessful Democratic primary challenger, Chris Davis.<ref name="WhoALNov" />

In the Republican primary, candidate Christian Horn officially withdrew from the race on February 25, 2024, leaving Robin Litaker as the only active candidate seeking the Republican nomination. ''[[1819 News]]'' reported that votes for Horn would not be certified, and that the [[Alabama Republican Party]] had been notified of Horn's withdrawal.<ref name="horn1" /> However, Horn won the primary on March 5 despite his withdrawal.<ref name="horn2">{{Cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/03/05/us/elections/results-alabama-us-house-7-primary.html|title=Alabama 7th Congressional District Primary Election Results|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=March 5, 2024|access-date=March 5, 2024}}</ref> The Alabama Republican Party later published a press release clarifying that Horn had dropped out of the race and Litaker would be the party's nominee in 2024.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.demopolistimes.com/2024/03/06/despite-winning-primary-horn-will-not-be-republican-nominee-after-being-allowed-to-withdraw-from-race-litaker-will-face-sewell-in-general-election/|title=Despite winning primary, Horn will not be Republican nominee after being allowed to withdraw from race. Litaker will face Sewell in general election.|work=The Demopolis Times|date=March 6, 2024|access-date=March 8, 2024}}</ref>

